# Lesson 4, Mike's idea (original L02-05 North-South, turned around): 3NT, cross to dummy twice and run the clubs through East for the queen.
title: Run the ten
id: L04-09
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.1, 3.5
student: S
board: 71
dealer: S
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  KQ7    A743   86     T943
  E  632    T862   AT3    Q62
  S  A54    KQ5    KJ72   KJ5
  W  JT98   J9     Q954   A87

auction:
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 2NT    = 8-9 points, balanced: inviting game
  E Pass
  S 3NT    = A maximum: accept
     not Pass: You have 17, the top of your range. Accept the invitation.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT, partner invited with 2NT, and with 17 you accepted. You are
  declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the
  first trick.

planning:
  winners: 7-10
  spades: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  hearts: 3 = The king, queen and ace. A fourth if they split 3-3.
  diamonds: 0-1 = Your king, if East has the ace and you lead toward it.
  clubs: 1-3 = One once the ace is gone. Two or three if East has the queen and you lead clubs from dummy.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Clubs offer the most. Which hand should lead them, and how many times can you get there?
play:
  1   W SJ  N SQ*  E S2  S S4
      note: Seven winners on top. Clubs can give two more if East has the queen and you lead them from dummy, twice.
      SQ key: Win in dummy, so you can lead a club right away. Dummy's ♠K is a second way across, and your ♠A stays home to stop the spades.
      SQ not S7: Did you think about winning in dummy? You need to lead clubs from there. Winning in your hand with the ♠A means using up another spade stopper later just to get across.
  2   N CT  E C2  S C5*  W CA
      note: Lead the ♣10 from dummy.
      C5 key: Let the ten run. If East has the queen, the ten pushes out an honor, and your king and jack sit over East's queen.
      C5 not *: Did you think about letting the ten run? Leading from dummy only works if you wait to see East's card. Play low and keep the king and jack behind East.
  3   W ST  N SK  E S3  S S5
      note: West won the ace and kept on with spades. Win in dummy with the king: just where you want to be, and your ace is still at home.
  4   N C9  E C6  S CJ*  W C7
      note: Lead clubs from dummy again.
      CJ key: East played low, so finesse the jack. If East has the queen, it wins.
      CJ not CK: Did you think about the finesse? West has already won the ace, so East probably holds the queen. Play the jack.
  5   S CK  W C8  N C3  E CQ
      note: The jack won. Cash the king, and East's queen falls. Dummy's last club is a winner.
  6   S H5  W H9  N HA  E H6
      note: Over to dummy's ♥A to cash the club.
  7   N C4  E H2  S D2  W S8
      note: The fourth club.
  8   N H3  E H8  S HK  W HJ
      note: Back to your hearts.
  9   S HQ  W D4  N H7  E HT
  10  S SA  W S9  N S7  E S6
      note: That's nine tricks.
  11  S D7  W D5  N D6  E DA
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  12  E D3  S DJ  W DQ  N D8
  13  W D9  N H4  E DT  S DK
      note: 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: count seven winners on top and get two more from the clubs, by
  leading them from dummy twice so that your king and jack sit over East's
  queen.

  Winning the first spade in dummy was the quiet key: you could lead a club
  at once, dummy's ♠K was a second way across, and your ♠A stayed home as a
  stopper. If the club finesse had lost, you would still have had chances:
  a 3-3 heart split, or a diamond led toward your king.
